Relevant tables, columns and how they will be used  

olist_order_items  
• order_id : to link every item to the payments that were made for its order  
• product_id : to link every item to its product information  

olist_products  
• product_id : join key to olist_order_items  
• product_category_name : the product category we need to analyse  

olist_order_payments  
• order_id : join key to olist_order_items (through the order)  
• payment_type : the payment method (“credit_card”, “voucher”, etc.) that we must count  

Typical join / filter conditions that may be useful  
• olist_order_items.order_id = olist_order_payments.order_id  
• olist_order_items.product_id = olist_products.product_id  
• You might optionally add “… WHERE payment_value > 0” or “WHERE order_status = 'delivered'” (if you decide to restrict the scope), but it is not mandatory for the basic counts requested here.  

Below are ten exploratory‑to‑final SQL queries (SQLite syntax).  
Every query is capped with LIMIT 20 so that you can inspect a small sample of rows before running the full queries on your side.

```sql
-- 1) Distinct list of payment types
SELECT DISTINCT "payment_type"
FROM "olist_order_payments"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Distinct list of product categories found in the catalogue
SELECT DISTINCT "product_category_name"
FROM "olist_products"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Categories with their English translation (when available)
SELECT DISTINCT p."product_category_name",
       t."product_category_name_english"
FROM "olist_products"  AS p
LEFT JOIN "product_category_name_translation" AS t
       ON p."product_category_name" = t."product_category_name"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Quick look at some order‑level payments
SELECT "order_id",
       "payment_sequential",
       "payment_type",
       "payment_installments",
       "payment_value"
FROM "olist_order_payments"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Map a few orders to the categories of the items they contain
SELECT i."order_id",
       i."product_id",
       p."product_category_name"
FROM "olist_order_items" AS i
JOIN "olist_products"    AS p
  ON i."product_id" = p."product_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Overall popularity of each payment type (top 20)
SELECT "payment_type",
       COUNT(*) AS "n_payments"
FROM "olist_order_payments"
GROUP BY "payment_type"
ORDER BY "n_payments" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Overall number of payments per product category (regardless of payment type)
SELECT p."product_category_name",
       COUNT(*) AS "n_payments"
FROM "olist_order_items" AS i
JOIN "olist_products"    AS p  ON i."product_id" = p."product_id"
JOIN "olist_order_payments" AS pay ON pay."order_id" = i."order_id"
GROUP BY p."product_category_name"
ORDER BY "n_payments" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Payments broken down by category AND payment type (top 20 combos)
SELECT p."product_category_name",
       pay."payment_type",
       COUNT(*) AS "n_payments"
FROM "olist_order_items" AS i
JOIN "olist_products"        AS p   ON p."product_id" = i."product_id"
JOIN "olist_order_payments"  AS pay ON pay."order_id" = i."order_id"
GROUP BY p."product_category_name", pay."payment_type"
ORDER BY "n_payments" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) For every category, keep only the payment type that is MOST USED in that category
--    (still limited to 20 rows for inspection)
WITH category_payment_counts AS (
    SELECT p."product_category_name",
           pay."payment_type",
           COUNT(*) AS "n_payments"
    FROM "olist_order_items"       AS i
    JOIN "olist_products"          AS p   ON p."product_id" = i."product_id"
    JOIN "olist_order_payments"    AS pay ON pay."order_id" = i."order_id"
    GROUP BY p."product_category_name", pay."payment_type"
),
category_top_payment AS (
    SELECT c1."product_category_name",
           c1."payment_type",
           c1."n_payments"
    FROM   category_payment_counts AS c1
    WHERE  c1."n_payments" = (
           SELECT MAX(c2."n_payments")
           FROM   category_payment_counts AS c2
           WHERE  c2."product_category_name" = c1."product_category_name")
)
SELECT *
FROM category_top_payment
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) FINAL ANSWER:
--     Top 3 product categories whose MOST‑USED payment type has the highest number of payments
WITH category_payment_counts AS (
    SELECT p."product_category_name",
           pay."payment_type",
           COUNT(*) AS "n_payments"
    FROM "olist_order_items"       AS i
    JOIN "olist_products"          AS p   ON p."product_id" = i."product_id"
    JOIN "olist_order_payments"    AS pay ON pay."order_id" = i."order_id"
    GROUP BY p."product_category_name", pay."payment_type"
),
category_top_payment AS (
    SELECT c1."product_category_name",
           c1."payment_type",
           c1."n_payments"
    FROM   category_payment_counts AS c1
    WHERE  c1."n_payments" = (
           SELECT MAX(c2."n_payments")
           FROM   category_payment_counts AS c2
           WHERE  c2."product_category_name" = c1."product_category_name")
)
SELECT "product_category_name",
       "payment_type",
       "n_payments"
FROM   category_top_payment
ORDER BY "n_payments" DESC
LIMIT 20;          -- will show the top‑3 rows among the 20‑row cap
```
